  "ime-message-drill-needshammer": "You need a hammer in your offhand to use the drill.",


  // ── Handbook entries ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "handbookentry-muckpile-title": "Muck",
  "handbookentry-muckpile-body": "Muck is the waste material produced when mining stone and ore. Every block you break produces Muck, which settles into piles. Left alone, muck piles spread into tailings mounds. Use a Muck Bag to carry it, and a Sluice or Crusher to extract value from it.",

  "handbookentry-muckbag-title": "Muck Bag",
  "handbookentry-muckbag-body": "A Muck Bag is the only way to carry Muck. Without one, muck stays where it fell. Higher-tier bags carry more. With four bags equipped, a player can move significantly more muck per trip. Muck cannot be stored in chests or crates.",


  "mining101-title": "Mining 101 - On Muck and Fortune",
  "mining101-body": "<strong>Mining 101 - On Muck and Fortune</strong><br><i>This will cover Muck, it's creation, types, and other important information regarding it's processing and technical details</i><br><br><i>All muck is modeled as Granite muck, due to performance issues. Ore types are still rendered on muck though!</i><br><br><strong>What is Muck and Where does it come from?</strong><br><br>Muck is a byproduct of mining stone with a pickaxe or blasting it with drilling and blasting. It is a unique, custom built pile system that allows multiple material types to be contained within 1 block. Muck blocks can contain up to 8 layers of muck, and slide and distribute similar to vanilla piles, except without using falling entities. This is because vanilla falling entities causes a host of issues with stability and layer deletion within the muck infrastructure, so I opted for custom pile structures.<br><br>Muck is dropped from stone and ore blocks, when breaking raw stone it produces between 5 and 7 layers of stone type muck. When breaking ore blocks it produces 1-4 layers of ore muck, and the remainder as stone muck. The stone muck is meant to be gangue that is seperated from the ore muck, a staple feature of IME. <br><br>Poor ore = 1 ore layer and 4-6 stone layers<br>Normal ore = 2 ore layers and 3-5 stone layers<br>Rich ore = 3 ore layers and 2-4 stone layers<br>Bountiful ore = 4 ore layers and 1-3 stone layers<br><br>Ore muck contains 2 major properties the player needs to know about. Concentration, and Availability. Concentration is the total metal units in the ore. All muck has a default amount of 25 Concentration of metal in it. (For metallic ores, nonmetallic ores are dealt with in a different way). Availability is a % amount of the total metal units you will recieve. Both numbers can be increased, with maximum concentration at 100 (one ingot) and maximum availability up to 1 (100%). Muck also comes in 3 different types, which effect Availability.<br><br>Here are the defaults(nonconfigurable):<br>Coarse Muck = Availability of 15% (0.15) and concentration of 25<br>Raw Muck = Availability of 25%(0.25) and concentration of 25<br>Fine Muck = Availability of 35%(0.35) and concentration of 25.<br><br>Keep in mind stone muck has no ore content, and cannot be extracted with such. You can obtain stones, sand, and gravel from various muck types from a Sieve(block). Covered in Mining 102 - A Catalogue of Contraptions, and refining ores will be covered in Mining 103 - In Which Fire is Persuaded to be Useful.<br><br><strong>Logistics of Muck</strong><br><br>To haul around muck you may notice there's no item. You will require a form of Muck bag, in one of 4 tiers. Rawhide, Linen, Leather, or Hardened Leather. To pick up muck break it with a shovel or Muck Shovel (that digs in a 3x3 area). There is also a Muck Bin you can mount on boats (and hopefully other mods that use similar attachment mechanics) and on the ground to store up to 32 layers of muck. Although I recommend leaving muck on the ground or processing it into sieves unless you intend on transporting or storing high grade ore.<br><br><strong>Things of Note:</strong><br>- When picking up or placing down muckpiles, it is intentionally random as to what layer or bag entry is moved.<br>- Mucks' built in sloughing system allows building of ore chutes and processing by water as well. (Covered in Mining 102).",
  "mining102-title": "Mining 102 - A Catalogue of Contraptions",
  "mining102-body": "<strong>Mining 102 - A Catalogue of Contraptions</strong><br><i>This covers the various processing mechanisms for Muck, powered and unpowered, and most importantly water processing</i><br><br><strong>Water Processing of Muck</strong><br><br>Muck unlike all other piles, has a built in feature that can seperate ore muck from stone muck decently efficiently. When submerged in water that is flowing (possibly including river and watershed mods) muck will tick every so often, between 5 and 15 seconds. Every time it ticks, it will move one layer from the muckpile downstream. The finer the muck the better it is at moving lights away, and faster it processes. Generally, heavier materials will move slower in water, and lighter materials will move faster, thereby automatically seperating these materials from each other over a relatively short span of time.<br><br><strong>Sluice Baffle</strong> is a block you can place under the flowing stream to collect ore type mucks, up to 800 total concentration. To remove muck from sluice baffles simply right click with a muck or normal shovel. It will deposit it's inventory 1 layer at a time using up to 100 units of concentration from within the baffle, all of the same type of ore. Fine muck has a higher chance of being captured by the baffle, as opposed to raw muck. And the sluice baffle cnnot process coarse muck. (Fine 60% capture chance, raw 35%)<br><br><strong>Mechanical Processing</strong><br>In rough order of progression of processing machines.<br><br><strong>Sieve</strong> block is the first major mechanical process you'll have access to. It processes muck layers and outputs a corrisponding output. For ore mucks it will drop 1 nugget per layer (which is VERY inefficient, but good in a pinch). For stone muck, it drops more stones for the coarser mucks, and more sand on the finer mucks, but broadly will produce stones, sand, and gravel for Raw. Input muck layers on top of the sieve. Any sand can also be placed in the top to generate small amounts of crushed quartz. Non-metallic mucks can be put into the top for a good yield of materials. This is their only processing method.<br><br><strong>Screen</strong> is the seperator, taking in all muck types and splitting Raw muck (at 75% coarse 25% fine). It outputs coarse muck forward, and fine muck below it. Input is the block above it (from the top)<br><br><strong>Crusher</strong> processes coarse muck and refines it into fine muck (75%) and raw muck (25%). Requires power (from IME's system).<br><br><strong>Mechanical Transportation of Muck</strong><br><i>Muck can be a lot to handle just in bags, so I've added various ways to move it up and sideways. Luckily, gravity takes care of down!</i><br><br><strong>Flat Conveyor</strong> moves muck that's directly on top of it in it's forward direction to the block ahead and up of it. (Requires IME power)<br><br><strong>Split Conveyor</strong> splits muck on top of it and moves the entire pile either left or right from it's forward direction. (Requires IME power)<br><br><strong>Bucket Lift</strong> is split into body and output, the body doesn't require anything, it just extends the bucket lift down. It takes from the block directly beneath the lowest body location in a pillar. The output puts to the same block height it is on (so one over) in the way it's facing. (The bucket lift output requires IME Power)<br><br><strong>Power Connector</strong> is for transferring power and connecting systems together. Keep in mind all IME power blocks can transfer power through them as well, so as long as all parts are touching each other's faces it will be powered.<br><br><strong>Power Input</strong> is the interface between vanilla mechanical power and IME's internal power system. This block has 1 input on one side for Mechanical power, and then 5 outputs on all of the rest to connect to IME's power systems and blocks.",
  "mining103-title": "Mining 103 - In Which Fire is Persuaded to Be Useful",
  "mining103-body": "<strong>Mining 103 - In Which Fire is Persuaded to Be Useful</strong><br><i>Concerning smelting, furnaces, and structures in matter of heat.</i><br><br>Broadly, IME introduces 3 new furnace types, with 3 variants each. Roasting furnaces, Smelting furnaces, and Blooming furnaces. Keep in mind all furnaces have doors that are their core block. Crouch + right click to show a blueprint projection of the structure similar to vanilla Beehive Kiln or Cementation Furnace, and crouch + click again to cycle the furnace tier. All fuel positions in furnaces need to have a fuel in them to work, and it takes the lowest temperature fuel as the furnace process temp. Furthermore no furnaces take damage, due to requiring building multiple of them during a playthough.<br><br><strong>Roasting Furnaces</strong><br>Roasting raises availability in the ore muck that is roasted in the furnace. You can only roast ore once, and it increases all ores availability (except Iron ores) by 35%(0.35). Iron is unique though, in that it increases it's availability to 65% for Coarse, 85% for Raw, and 100% for Fine mucks. Roasting Iron ore is the only way to obtain the maximum yield on it's specific ore type.<br><br>Roasting doesn't require extremely high temperatures, in fact only requiring 600c to roast ores, once up to temperature it takes 8 hours of holding. Temperature ramp up scales with the size of the furnace. <br><br>Roasting furnaces can be made in 3 tiers, all with the same materials available at every tier. Clay bricks, fire clay brick variants such as brickcourse, and refractory bricks are all valid, but roasting furnaces always require grating made of at least firebrick.<br><br><strong>Smelting Furnaces</strong><br> Smelting furnaces are the big step from Muck to Metal and are probably the more complex of the available furnaces in IME. Smelting Furnaces use the Smeltery Door for their base, an early game mudbrick craft. Tier 1 & 2 Smelting furnaces don't use grates, but tier 3 does, and requires it to be made of at least fireclay bricks.<br>Tier 1: Mudbrick / Clay Brick (including fire) / Refractory Brick<br>Tier 2: Clay Brick / Refractory Brick<br>Tier 3: Fire Clay Brick / Refractory Brick<br><br>Smelting furnaces have locations for Smelt Pots. 1 location just under the door for T1, 4 locations under and to the back of the door for Tier 2, and all on top of the grates for Tier 3 (9 locations). Fuel locations for T1 and T2 are generally right next to the smelt pots. Each full sized smelting pot can hold up to 32 layers of muck and up to 1 full stack each of the various kinds of fluxes available for IME. The small smelt pot can only hold 16 layers but can be made of red/blue clay and has only capacity for 4 stacks of various flux types. List of fluxes and their availability bonuses:<br><br>Powdered Borax = +10%<br>Lime = +10%<br>Crushed Quartz = +10%Powdered Charcoal = +8%<br><br>Potash = +6%<br>Charcoal Piece = +5%<br>Powdered Flint = +3%<br>Powdered Iron Oxide = +3%<br><br>The Smelting furnace is designed for any non-ferrous ore, the complete list is Copper, Tin, Bismuth, Zinc, Lead, Silver, Gold, and Platinum. When preparing a charge for the Smelt Pots it's good to keep in mind stone muck is wasted space. It requires flux layers and overall makes the smelt slightly poorer (in space not in actual effect). Stone muck is deleted during smelts and when smelting is done it outputs Pig Metal ingots, which are simply melted back down in a crucible like vanilla. A big thing to keep in mind is 1 flux is required (for full efficiency) per 4 layers of muck to be processed. If there's less flux than that, you wont get the full benefit of the flux additive.<br><br>Smelting ore actually requires a fuel hot enough to melt the base metal. For example you can refine Zinc with firewood, but you have  to refine copper with at least a coal or better. The furnace automatically takes the burn temperature of the pile in the fuel location(s) and applies that to the whole furnace. <br><br>Smelting Furnaces have ramp up times relative to their size, so Tier 1 has 1 hour ramp, 8 hour hold, 1 hour cool, Tier 2 has a 2 hour ramp, 8 hour heat, 2 hour cool, and tier 3 has a 4 hour heat, 8 hour hold, and 4 hour cool. During the cooling stage of the furnaces all fuel needs to be removed from the furnaces and they need to be let to cool slowly.<br><br>Finally, there is a unique mechanic with crucibles, if you crouch right click a crucible full of molten metal on a barrel of water, and the barrel has nothing in the inventory slot, it will create an item called Prill which is single unit nuggets of metal, to preserve all metal from the pig ingot smelt.<br><br><strong>Bloomery Furnaces(High Temp Furnace)</strong><br>These are designed for (currently) Iron only materials. Hematite, Magnetite, and Limonite from Vanilla, and Pyrite from Geology Additions currently work as valid muck pile inputs.<br><br>Large Bloomeries are a lot simpler than the other furnaces. Simply fill the furnace with Muck and drop charcoal or coal coke into the top of it, and then right click the door with a torch (not crouching). Charcoal gives the furnace 1 fuel unit, while coal coke gives 2 units. A Bloomery requires the following fuel units per layers of muck:<br><br>Tier 1: 1 fuel for 2 layers of muck<br>Tier 2: 1 fuel for 4 layers of muck<br>Tier 3: 1 fuel for 6 layers of muck<br><br>All 3 tiers can only be made of firebrick. Not refractory, nor clay bricks or mudbricks, but firebrick ONLY. Each brick in the bloomery has a 5% chance of breaking after every firing, this is the ONLY FURNACE THAT CAN BREAK!<br><br>When the smelt is over and the bloomery has finished processing, inside of the furnace you'll find an Iron Mass. Break this with a pickaxe and it will drop 1 Iron Bloom per 100 units of Iron, and then 1 iron nugget for every excess unit of iron beyond 5.<br><br><i>The only way to get Bloomery furnaces is to have iron already, so you must use a Sieve on Iron Ore, and process a bit of Iron in the vanilla way to get the one of these. They're far more efficient in material production.</i>",
  "mining104-title": "Mining 104 - A Short Argument with Stone",
  "mining104-body": "<strong>Mining 104 - A Short Argument with Stone</strong><br><i>Concerning drilling, powder, plugs, fuses, and the many ways a hole may decide to become a lesson.</i><br><br>Drilling in IME is a deliberate little minigame, and it rewards doing things in the proper order. A drill hole begins at 0 blocks deep. To advance it, strike the drill first and then turn it (Right and Left click). Each successful hit and turn cycle advances the hole by 1 full block. If you try to turn before striking, nothing happens. Drill angle matters as well, so make sure the tool is set to the angle you actually want before continuing. You need to be not looking at a block to change vertical angle (small quirk of the code). Horizontal angle is the angle you are looking forward.<br><br>Different drill lengths are meant for different depths. Short Hand Drills are for 0 to 4 block holes. Medium Hand drills continue from 4 to 8 blocks. Long hand drills continue from 8 to 12 blocks. Bronze/Iron/Steel is the rough progression for each drill type. Once a hole reaches the limit of the current drill, you must change to a longer one to continue. After every successful turn the hole becomes dirty again, which means it must be cleaned before it can be loaded with powder.<br><br><strong>Cleaning and Preparing the Hole</strong><br><br>Use the Blowpipe to clean drilled holes. A hole must be at least 1 block deep before it can be cleaned and loaded. Burnt out and spent holes also need to be cleaned before they can be reused. Cleaning is not optional. If a hole is dirty, it cannot be prepared properly.<br><br>Once a hole is clean, load it with Blasting Powder. Powder requirements are based on depth. You need 1 powder per 4 blocks of hole depth, rounded up. A 1 to 4 block hole needs 1 powder, a 5 to 8 block hole needs 2 powder, and a 9 to 12 block hole needs 3 powder. When enough powder has been loaded, use a Clay Plug to seal the hole.<br><br><strong>Fuses and Fire</strong><br><br>After plugging the hole, use the Fuse Awl to install blasting fuse. The awl sets the fuse time, and longer delays consume more fuse. Once fused, the hole can be ignited with a torch. Quickmatch and connected fuse runs can also be lit, which allows several prepared shots to be fired in sequence if the system has been laid out correctly.<br><br><strong>How the Blast Travels</strong><br><br>IME blasting does not behave like one large instant explosion. Instead, the blast travels down the drilled hole in short steps. This makes it much better at following the shape and direction of the hole, and much less likely to feel like a vague magical burst. The standard blast radius is 0.75 blocks, and the blast can detect nearby valid holes as blast into paths, including ones that have not yet been cleaned.<br><br>Blasting is powerful, but it is not perfectly obedient. Hangfires, duds, hard failures, and gas cuts can all occur. Gas cuts may partially consume the charge. Failed shots and spent holes will usually leave the hole burnt out, and a burnt out hole must be cleaned again before reuse. In practice, blasting rewards planning, patience, and a respectful fear of what you have just stuffed into the wall.<br><br><strong>Practical Advice</strong><br><br>Drill with the final direction of the blast in mind. Clean every hole before loading it. Check that the powder count matches the depth. Plug the hole before fusing it. Use longer fuse times when working with several connected charges. Most importantly, remember what blasting is for. It is the fast, loud, violent way to turn a lot of rock into a great deal of muck, and it is usually worth the trouble so long as you are the one conducting the argument instead of the stone."
}
